{
  "concept": "A line integral is defined by partitioning a curve into small segments, approximating the mass of each using a sample point, and summing these values; as the segments shrink, this Riemann sum converges to the exact integral.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Main curve path, rendered as a blue line with a loop",
    "Partition points P_0 through P_n drawn as blue dots on the main curve",
    "Labels for points P=P_0, P_1, P_2, P_3, P_{k-1}, P_k, P_{n-1}, Q=P_n",
    "Highlight or tick marks isolating the k-th arc segment on the main curve",
    "Dimension lines and label for arc length \\Delta s_k on the main curve",
    "Text label 'Mass \\Delta M_k' pointing to the k-th segment on the main curve",
    "Large, light-orange curved zoom arrow pointing from the main curve to the inset",
    "Magnified inset showing the k-th arc segment as a larger blue line",
    "Points P_{k-1} and P_k as blue dots bounding the inset segment",
    "Dimension lines and label for \\Delta s_k on the inset segment",
    "Red dot representing the sample point P_k^* on the inset segment",
    "Label P_k^*(x_k^*, y_k^*, z_k^*) next to the red sample point",
    "Formula box at the bottom reading '\\Delta M_k \\approx f(x_k^*, y_k^*, z_k^*) \\Delta s_k'",
    "Text readout displaying a Riemann sum value that converges as n increases"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "numSegments",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Number of partitions (n)",
      "range": [4, 50, 1],
      "default": 8,
      "teaches": "Increasing the number of segments shrinks the length of each arc, refining the Riemann sum approximation towards the true continuous line integral."
    },
    {
      "id": "samplePos",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Sample point position",
      "range": [0, 1, 0.01],
      "default": 0.5,
      "teaches": "Moving the sample point changes the evaluated density for a specific segment, but as segments become infinitesimally small, the exact placement within the segment ceases to affect the final sum."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Partitioning the curve",
      "narration": "The curve from P to Q is divided into n sub-arcs. We estimate the total mass by approximating the mass of each sub-arc and summing them.",
      "state": {
        "numSegments": 8,
        "samplePos": 0.5
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Choosing a sample point",
      "narration": "Within each segment k, we pick a sample point to evaluate the density function. Slide the sample point position to see it move along the magnified arc segment.",
      "state": {
        "numSegments": 8,
        "samplePos": 0.8
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Convergence",
      "narration": "As the number of partitions n approaches infinity, the length of each segment approaches zero, and the sum converges to the exact line integral regardless of sample point placement.",
      "state": {
        "numSegments": 50,
        "samplePos": 0.8
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The exact line integral is found by breaking the curve into infinitely many pieces, where the sub-arcs become so small that the variation of the function within any single piece is negligible.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Draw the main curve as a smooth blue Bezier path with a distinctive loop. Distribute the partition points (blue dots) evenly along the curve based on the 'numSegments' slider. Keep the k-th segment visually anchored (e.g., around halfway along the path) so the zoom arrow always makes sense. The zoom arrow should be a thick, semi-transparent pale orange path with an arrowhead. In the magnified view, the red sample point's position along the blue arc segment is directly driven by the 'samplePos' slider (0 = P_{k-1}, 1 = P_k). Maintain math italicization for variables in the labels."
}

